<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fourvière</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fourvière is a hill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, overlooking the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a>. The Roman colony of Lugdunum was founded on it in 43 BCE, and the remains of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and odeon survive on the slope. The hill is crowned by the Basilica of Notre-Dame de Fourvière, built from 1872.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fourviere.jpg" alt="Fourvière" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gonedelyon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_vierge_de_Fourvi%C3%A8re.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
